**Coronary heart disease** (CHD) is when the **coronary arteries**, which carry blood to your heart muscle, become **narrowed by fatty deposits**. This means your heart may not get enough blood and oxygen, especially when it has to work harder. It is one of the most common causes of death in the UK, but it can be treated and managed.

{% callout type="danger" title="Heart attack: call 999 straight away if you have" %}
- **chest pain** that feels like pressure, squeezing or tightness, and does not go away
- pain that **spreads** to your arms, neck, jaw, back or tummy
- **sweating**, feeling **sick** or **breathless**, or feeling light-headed

Some people, especially women, older people and people with diabetes, have milder or less typical symptoms. If in doubt, call 999. The call handler may tell you to chew an aspirin.
{% /callout %}

## Symptoms

Some people have no symptoms until they have a heart attack. Others have:

- **angina:** a tight, heavy or dull pain in the chest, often brought on by activity, cold weather or stress, which eases with rest
- **breathlessness**
- **tiredness** when doing things you used to manage

## Causes and risk factors

The main things that raise your risk are:

- **smoking**
- **high blood pressure**
- **high cholesterol**
- **diabetes**
- being **overweight** or **inactive**
- **drinking too much alcohol**
- a **family history** of heart disease at a young age
- being **older**, or **male**
- being of **South Asian** heritage

Many of these can be changed, which is why healthy changes make such a difference.

## Tests

Your GP may refer you to a **rapid access chest pain clinic** or a heart specialist. Tests can include:

- an **ECG**, a painless heart trace
- **blood tests**
- a **CT scan** of the heart's arteries
- an **angiogram**, where dye is used to show the arteries on X-ray
- tests to see how your heart works when it is **under stress**

## Treatment

Treatment includes medicines, healthy changes and, for some people, procedures to open or bypass the narrowed arteries. See [coronary heart disease treatment](/conditions/physical/coronary-heart-disease/treatment/).
